# CODEOWNERS for the knowledge workspace.
#
# Format: each line maps a path pattern to one or more GitHub usernames or teams.
# Patterns follow gitignore-style syntax. Last matching pattern wins, so
# more-specific rules (teams) must come AFTER less-specific ones (departments).
#
# Owners must have write or admin access on the repo, or GitHub silently
# ignores the entry. Multiple owners on one line = any one of them can satisfy
# a required review. Use `@your-org/team-slug` to route to a GitHub team instead
# of an individual.
#
# GitHub never requests a review from the PR author and never falls back to
# an earlier pattern, so a line whose only owner is the author would request
# nobody at all. Team lines carry the department lead as the second owner to
# cover that case. Department lines carry only the department lead: the
# codeowners-fallback workflow requests a review from the owners on the `*`
# default line when (and only when) the PR author is the sole owner of a
# changed file, so leads' own PRs still get a reviewer without the fallbacks
# being requested on every department PR.
# That workflow only understands the pattern forms used below (`*`, `/exact/file`,
# `/dir/` prefix, `/dir/**/file` glob) - keep new lines in those forms. A form it
# does not understand fails silently: GitHub honors the line, the workflow skips
# it, and the two then disagree about who owns the file.
#
# Handles below are placeholders prefixed with `@TODO-`. Search-and-replace
# them with real GitHub handles. Lines with unresolved `@TODO-` handles are
# silently ignored by GitHub, so partial fills are safe to land. The
# `setup-workspace` skill fills them in from the interview.
#
# Auto-request only by default. To require approval before merge, enable
# "Require review from Code Owners" in branch protection for `main`.

